  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1223 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nWV imagery indicates a west-northwest flow aloft prevailing \nacross the Western High Plains downstream of a flattening ridge\naxis situated across the Four Corners Region. Near the surface,\nan area of low pressure remains anchored in southeast Colorado.\n\nRelatively dry conditions are likely for much of the area early \nin the period as the SREF shows an upper level high continuing \nto dominate the Four Corners Region through late Tuesday before \nfinally dislodging eastward into the Central Plains mid-week. \nHowever, a similar set up from yesterday still exists with \nisolated thunderstorms likely developing across the high plains \nof eastern Colorado late afternoon as yet another upper level \nshortwave ejects out of the northern Colorado Rockies. \nSufficient mid-level moisture/instability present combined with \nsteepening mid-level lapse rates will set the stage for \nthunderstorm development as ejecting H5 vort maxima interact \nwith a trough axis/stalled boundary in northeast Colorado. A few\nstray storms drifting into west central and extreme southwest \nKansas cannot be ruled out later this evening as suggested by \nsome CAMs. After a mainly dry Tuesday, very minimal thunderstorm\nchances (20%) return to west central/extreme southwest Kansas \nlate Wednesday as an upper level shortwave trough climbs \nnortheast across the high plains of eastern Colorado on the \nbackside of a ridge axis departing through the Central Plains.\n\nAnother warm night is expected tonight as prevailing southerlies \ncontinue to reinforce a warmer air mass across southwest/central \nKansas. With the HREF showing a widespread 70-90% probability of \ntemperatures dropping below 80F, look for lows mainly down into \nthe 70s(F). Well above normal temperatures are likely Tuesday \nand Wednesday with ridging aloft transitioning slowly east \nthrough the region and southerlies prevailing across \nsouthwest/central Kansas, supporting H85 temperatures a little \nabove 30C in central Kansas to near 35C near the Colorado line. \nExpect afternoon highs up around to a little above 100F both \ndays with the HREF painting a widespread 70- 90% probability of \ntemperatures exceeding 100F.",
